      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "3. Why can't we write only numbers in a for loop, and why do we declare a variable such as i?",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "The compiler does not understand plain numbers such as 1 and 5 in the loop header, so it reports an error. The starting value has to be stored in a variable, and the type is mentioned, here int because it is a whole number. The variable is conventionally n",
          "bold": false,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        },
        {
          "text": "amed i (for iteration), but any valid variable name can be used.",
          "bold": false,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "text": "for (1; 5; ++) {                  // compile-time error\n    System.out.println(\"StringStack\");\n}\n\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {    // correct\n    System.out.println(\"StringStack\");\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "4. Exp",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        },
        {
          "text": "lain the role of initialization, condition and increment/decrement with the example for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++).",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "text": "int i = 1 is the initialization: the loop starts from 1. i <= 5 is the condition: the loop continues while i is less than or equal to 5, so it goes until 5. i++ is the increment: i increases by 1 after each repetition, so the loop moves forward. To start from a different number, change the initialization; to stop earlier or later, change the condition; to move backward, use a decrement."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "5. Write",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        },
        {
          "text": " a program to print the numbers from 1 to 10.",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Instead of printing a fixed string, print the value of the loop variable. Its value changes in every repetition.",
          "bold": false,
