    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "7. What happens if the condition or the direction is wrong?",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "text": "If the condition is false at the start, the loop body never executes. If the direction does not move towards the end of the condition, the loop does not finish in a reasonable time. So the condition must match the direction: use <= (or <) when the value increases and >= (or >) when it decreases, and always keep the increment/decrement consistent with it.\nfor (int i = 10; i <= 1; i--) {   // condition is false at the start: nothing is printed\n    System.out.println(i);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "8. What is a compound assignment operator?",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "A compound assignment operator combines an arithmetic operation with assignment. i++ is the same as i = i + 1. In the same way, i += 2 is the same as i = i + 2, and i -= 2 is the same as i = i - 2. They are used w",
          "bold": false,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        },
        {
          "text": "hen the value must change by a number other than 1.",
          "bold": false,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "9. Write a program to print 20, 18, 16, ... down to 2 (skipping the odd numbers).",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "text": "Start from 20, continue while i >= 2, and decrease by 2 on each repetition using the compound assignment operator i -= 2 (which means i = i - 2).\npublic class Demo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (int i = 20; i >= 2; i -= 2) {\n            System.out.println(i);\n        }\n    }\n}"
