      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "10. Why should you solve looping problems by first identifying the start, end and direction?",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Every looping problem can be converted into a for loop once you know the three things: where to start, where to go, and whether to increase or decrease. For exampl",
          "bold": false,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        },
        {
          "text": "e, printing 1 to 10 starts at 1 and ends at 10 with an increase; printing 10 to 1 starts at 10 and ends at 1 with a decrease; printing 20 to 2 with only even numbers starts at 20, ends at 2 and decreases by 2. Write these down first, then convert them into",
          "bold": false,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        },
        {
          "text": " the three parts of the for loop.",
          "bold": false,
          "italic": false,
          "underline": false,
          "mono": false
        }
      ],
      "align": "left",
      "indent": false
    }
